# Git – Complete Exercise Summary

## Part 1 – Create a Git Repository

**1.1. Create a folder called `my-first-repo`:**
```bash
mkdir my-first-repo
```

**1.2. Navigate into that folder:**
```bash
cd my-first-repo
```

**1.3. Initialize a Git repository:**
```bash
git init
```

**1.4. Create a file called `readme.txt`:**
```bash
touch readme.txt
```

## Part 2 – Basic Git Commands

**2.1. What command shows the current state of your repository?**
```bash
git status
```

**2.2. What command stages `readme.txt` for commit?**
```bash
git add readme.txt
```

**2.3. What command commits with the message "Add readme file"?**
```bash
git commit -m "Add readme file"
```

**2.4. What command shows your commit history?**
```bash
git log
```

## Part 3 – Modify a File

**3.1. Edit `readme.txt` and add a new line of text:**
```bash
nano readme.txt
```

**3.2. What does `git status` show now? Describe in your own words.**

The file `readme.txt` is modified. Git detects changes that have not yet been staged or committed.

**3.3. Stage and commit your changes with an appropriate message:**
```bash
git add readme.txt
git commit -m "Update readme file"
```

**3.4. How many commits do you have now?**

I have 2 commits: the first one created the README file, and the second one updated it.

## Part 4 – Branches

**4.1. What command lists all branches in your repository?**
```bash
git branch
```

**4.2. What command creates a new branch called `feature-script`?**
```bash
git branch feature-script
```

**4.3. What command switches to the `feature-script` branch?**
```bash
git switch feature-script
```

**4.4. What single command creates and switches to a new branch called `dev`?**
```bash
git switch -c dev
```

**4.5. Switch back to the `feature-script` branch:**
```bash
git switch feature-script
```

**4.6. Verify you are on the correct branch:**
```bash
git branch
```

The asterisk (*) indicates the current branch.

## Part 5 – Create an Installation Script

**5.1. Make sure you are on the `feature-script` branch:**
```bash
git switch feature-script
```

**5.2. Create a new file called `install.sh`:**
```bash
touch install.sh
```

**5.3. Add the following content to your script:**

```bash
#!/bin/bash

echo "Starting installation..."

sudo apt-get update

sudo apt-get install -y curl

echo "Installation complete!"
```

**5.4. Make the script executable:**
```bash
chmod +x install.sh
```

**5.5. Stage and commit your script with the message "Add install script":**
```bash
git add install.sh
git commit -m "Add install script"
```

**5.6. Check your commit history on this branch:**
```bash
git log --oneline
```

## Part 8 – Merge Branches

**8.1. Switch back to the `main` branch:**
```bash
git switch main
```

**8.2. List the files in your directory. Is `install.sh` present? Why or why not?**

No, `install.sh` is not present because it was created on the `feature-script` branch. The `main` branch has not received those changes yet.

Command:
```bash
ls
```

**8.3. What command merges `feature-script` into `main`?**
```bash
git merge feature-script
```

**8.4. List the files again. What changed?**

The `install.sh` file is now present because the `feature-script` branch has been merged into `main`.

Command:
```bash
ls
```

**8.5. Check your commit history. What do you observe?**

The commit history shows the changes made on the feature branch and their integration into `main`.

Command:
```bash
git log --oneline --graph --all
```

**8.6. What command deletes the `feature-script` branch after merging?**
```bash
git branch -d feature-script
```

## Part 9 – Push to GitHub

**9.1. Create a new GitHub repository called `my-first-repo`, without initializing it with a README file.**

**9.2. What command links your local repository to GitHub?**
```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/my-first-repo.git
```

**9.3. What command pushes your commits to GitHub?**
```bash
git push -u origin main
```

**9.4. Refresh your GitHub page. What do you see?**

I can see my project files, including `readme.txt` and `install.sh`. My local Git repository has been successfully uploaded to GitHub.

## Part 10 – Delete and Clone

**10.1. Navigate out of your project folder:**
```bash
cd ..
```

**10.2. What command deletes the local repository folder?**
```bash
rm -rf my-first-repo
```

**10.3. What command clones your repository from GitHub?**
```bash
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/my-first-repo.git
```

**10.4. Navigate into the cloned folder and verify your files are there:**
```bash
cd my-first-repo
ls
git status
```
